ABOUT:

    This directory contains a number of source files modified to duplicate Oscar Agertz's Ramses patch shared by him with me and fully self-contained in:

    /Users/tepper/codes/ramses_agertz/ramses

    The idea is to merge his changes with the most updated Ramses version and keep it that way as Ramses evolves. See (*) below.

    Note that the sub-folder 'dice' and 'dice_restart' contain their own respective README and they are thus omitted at this level.

    The file src_orig_list.txt (created with retrieve_src.sh) contains a list of the files WITH THEIR FULL PATHS in here less sub-directories and their content, created for convenience. These files are automatically retrieved and copied into src_orig.

    The file condinit.f90_fgas10_ramses_fork is needed to run model 'fgas10_ramses_fork'. To this end, see instructions in header of NML file namelist/fgas10_ramses_fork.nml


BUGS:
    - feedback.f90/ subroutine 'kinetic_feedback':
        variable 'ZSN' is hardcoded to use the first entry in 'zp', i.e. zp(...,1); not sure if this is correct!

IMPORTANT:

	1) These patches DO NOT SPLIT all if-clauses of the form:

		if(star.or.sink)then
			if(metal)then
			endif
		endif
	into:
		if(star.or.sink)then
			...
		endif
		if(metal)then
			...
		endif

	as does my dice patch in, e.g. particle_tree.f90.

TO DO:
    - Make feedback.f90 compatible with main branch. For now, it has been copied as is from src_orig as is


(*) REQUIRED STEPS:

    1) From:
            /Users/tepper/codes/ramses_agertz/ramses/
        copy the relevant source files into
            /Users/tepper/codes/ramses_fork/patch/tepper/oagertz/src_orig
        These are automatically retrieved and copied into src_orig by script retrieve_src.sh. See the latter for an full explanation.
        The file src_orig_list.txt contains a list of these FILES WITH THEIR FULL PATHS, useful to complete the next steps.


    2) From:
            /Users/tepper/codes/ramses_fork/
        OR
            /Users/tepper/codes/ramses_fork/patch/tepper
        copy the corresponding source files; ideally, these are from the most recent Ramses version. Probably THE BEST APPROACH is to do this one by one, together with 3 below.
        IMPORTANT: My patched files have PRIORITY over their standard copies!
        Make sure to check if the file under consideration is present in my patches! To check if the file under consideration is present in my patches, see content of file mypatches_list.txt (generated by mypatches_list.txt)


    3) Merge corresponding files using FileMerge

        On a file by file basis:

        3.1 Compare the corresponding files in
            /Users/tepper/codes/ramses_fork_29JUL2025/patch/tepper/oagertz
        and
            /Users/tepper/codes/ramses_fork_29JUL2025/
        OR
            /Users/tepper/codes/ramses_fork_29JUL2025/patch/tepper
        to find out the relevant changes/differences in the former with respect to the latter. It is recommended to open the former on the left, the latter on the right, of FileMerge.
        IMPORTANT: My patched files have PRIORITY over their standard copies!
        This means that if file xx.f90 is in any of my patches, this should be copied. To check if the file under consideration is present in my patches, see content of file mypatches_list.txt (generated by mypatches_list.txt)

        3.2 Compare the corresponding files in
            /Users/tepper/codes/ramses_fork/patch/tepper/oagertz/src_orig
        and
            /Users/tepper/codes/ramses_fork/patch/tepper/oagertz/
        copied in step 3.1, and merge into the latter the differences found in 3.1. It is recommended to open the former on the left, the latter on the right, of FileMerge.

        3.3 Compare the corresponding files in
            /Users/tepper/codes/ramses_fork/patch/tepper/oagertz/
        and
            /Users/tepper/codes/ramses_fork/
        OR
            /Users/tepper/codes/ramses_fork/patch/tepper
        and make sure they only differ by the same bits found in 3.1. It is recommended to open the former on the left, the latter on the right, of FileMerge.
        IMPORTANT: My patched files have PRIORITY over their standard copies!
        This means that if file xx.f90 is in any of my patches, this should be copied. To check if the file under consideration is present in my patches, see content of file mypatches_list.txt (generated by mypatches_list.txt)

